#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091943 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092189 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e61d3403-80d9-5633-b579-dcf452dbc265" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Template"}] :question/id #uuid "493f9465-dfd4-5103-8aba-e02d92282172" :question/math-academy-id "e-12674" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n> $2^n > 2n$ for all $n \\geq 3, n\\in\\mathbb N$\n\nWhat are the missing entries in the proof template outlined below?\n\n> **Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $000000$\n> $0000$\n> **Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $000000$ We have\n> $000000$\n> **Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n> $000000$\n> **Conclusion**: Therefore, $2^n > 2n$ for all $n \\geq 3$." :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step1**: Base Case\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n2^n > 2n\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case**. Since we are required to prove the statement for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 3\n$$\n\nthe base case is $P(3)$. So, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n> **Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}n=3}$.\n> $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2^3} > \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2(3)}$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show that the inequality holds.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some\n\n$$\nn = k \\geq 3\n$$\n\nTo do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n)$.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n> **Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}k \\geq 3}$, we have\n> $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2^k} > \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2k}$\n\nThen, we move on to the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$. We start by writing down $P(k+1)$.\n\n> **Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n> $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2^{k+1}} > \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2(k+1)}$.\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this inequality using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$, the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n> **Conclusion**: Therefore, $2^n > 2n$ for all $n \\geq 3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091949 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b83c640f-c7fd-5adc-94b8-b129d357b140" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Polynomial Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "6b8b1014-56bf-51c0-be9e-a194181a0d99" :question/math-academy-id "e-13225" :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that $n^2 > 2n+2$ for all $n \\geq 3, n\\in \\mathbb N$." :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3),\\ldots$, where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n- $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\nn^2 > 2n+2 \\quad\\text{for}\\quad n \\geq 3\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case. Since we are required to prove the statement for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 3\n$$\n\nthe base case is $P(3)$. So, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\n> We proceed using induction on $n$.\n> **Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=3$.\n> For the left-hand side, we have\n> $3^{2} = 9$,\n> and for the right-hand side, we have\n> $2(3) + 2 = 8$,\n> Thus, our statement is true for $n=3$.\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some\n\n$$\nn=k\\geq 3\n$$\n\n> **Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 3$, we have\n> $k^2 > 2k+2$.\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$. Let's start by writing down $P(k+1)$. This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n> **Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n> $(k+1)^2 > 2(k+1)+2. \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\n> We write\n> $(k + 1)^{2}|= k^{2} + 2k + 1$.\n> By the inductive hypothesis, we have\n> $k^{2} + 2k + 1|= (k^{2})_(⏟)_(> 2k + 2) + 2k + 1; > 2k + 2 + 2k + 1; = 2k + 2 + 2k_(⏟)_(> 1) + 1; > 2(k + 1) + 2$,\n> as required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is greater than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, by the principle of induction, $n^2 > 2n+2$ for all $n \\geq 3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091950 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82830730-a9bc-57bd-9114-d0d50473ea63" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Other Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "f5d8d9e3-d45c-5ad9-aae7-7933e6f4fade" :question/math-academy-id "e-13227" :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that $2^n +1 \\leq 3^n$ for $n\\in\\mathbb N$." :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3), \\ldots$, where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n- $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k \\in \\mathbb N$.\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n2^n +1 \\leq 3^n \\quad\\text{for} \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1)$. To do this, we substitute $n = 1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is less than or equal to the right-hand side:\n\n> We proceed using induction on $n$.\n> **Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n = 1$.\n> For the left-hand side, we have\n> $2^{1} + 1 = 3$,\n> and for the right-hand side, we have\n> $3^{1} = 3$,\n> Thus, our statement is true for $n = 1$.\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some\n\n$$\nn = k \\geq 1\n$$\n\n> **Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = k \\geq 1$, we have\n> $2^k +1 \\leq 3^k$.\n\nWe must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$. Let's start by writing down $P(k+1)$. This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n> **Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n> $2^{k+1} + 1\\leq 3^{k+1}. \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is less than or equal to the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\n> We write\n> $2^{k + 1} + 1|= 2^{k} \\cdot 2^{1} + 1; = 2(2^{k}) + 1; = 2^{k} + 2^{k} + 1$.\n> By the inductive hypothesis, we have\n> $2^{k} + 2^{k} + 1_(⏟)_(\\le 3^{k}) \\mid \\le 2^{k} + 3^{k}; = 2^{k}_(⏟)_(< 3^{k} < 2 \\cdot 3^{k}) + 3^{k}; < 2 \\cdot 3^{k} + 3^{k}; = 3 \\cdot 3^{k}; = 3^{k + 1}$,\n> as required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is less than or equal to the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, by the principle of induction, $2^n +1 \\leq 3^n$ for all $n \\geq 1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b83c640f-c7fd-5adc-94b8-b129d357b140" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Polynomial Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99ebb4a8-9428-5176-87d5-0323e4e2ca13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089968} {:answer/id #uuid "083ceab8-7b48-5ad2-a20a-a3e419b8063e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089969}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ae45a31e-7f58-5b7b-990d-8bd5b559d995" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089967} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2a479cf-57b7-588c-ab4e-c6d460c4dc23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089971} {:answer/id #uuid "f60a5848-f5ca-53eb-ac73-e452ed7c2a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089972} {:answer/id #uuid "500acc65-ce0c-5269-af57-a439d91f9730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(3 + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089973}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dd1f267e-4dfd-5c4c-82f9-ca25deeabef7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089970} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab3edad5-fac2-5c36-a745-82740036b12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089975} {:answer/id #uuid "c58ef623-b5b7-5a3b-babb-b7ac7b5cee15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089976} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6ae379-7afd-56cc-b170-5842774fba93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089977}] :answer-field/id #uuid "eeaa4049-257c-5000-8c05-dbc2bc2e541f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089974} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e32ed80f-6660-5c9a-82ae-0dcc0a4e0f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089979} {:answer/id #uuid "59126649-2842-565d-9ad6-f6cb17b72dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089980} {:answer/id #uuid "660384a1-823d-58de-ada5-b7ce9db11f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089981}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8c5e7f77-3f25-5126-8f63-eef907893f01" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089978} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3fb07b2-242b-5505-8017-d095b7160741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "less than" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089983} {:answer/id #uuid "65788e60-7fd3-5a45-8c92-f7efaf59b5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "greater than" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089984}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ca561fb4-c5ec-5da1-96d4-198edd64ea7c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089982}] :question/id #uuid "e55b1bb6-4fdf-5aaa-8b3c-9ba7f8dbcdd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-183786" :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that $6(n + 1) < n^{3}$ for $n \\ge 3, n ∈ N$.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n$.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n$6^{2}$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n = 3$."}]}
